Z||000002||rogergarrison||08-28-2001||03:46 AM||||Its my fav looking Porsche. The front engine V8 rwd didnt go over too good with Porsche buyers and had a fairly quick death. Also had quite a few misc mechanical problems with engines. But that all could be a good thing because there pretty affordable and plentyful as used cars. Ive seen pretty decent ones around for less than $8-10 K.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87ECO5||08-28-2001||05:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>The front engine V8 rwd didnt go over too good with Porsche buyers and had a fairly quick death.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Why is that?  The 944 had a similar configuration (smaller engine) and was a hit, wasn't it?<P>Could it be that the 928 failed with customers because it was to replace the 911 but didn't have the same rear-engine config?||||reg||5
Z||000004||Kasdan||08-28-2001||07:08 AM||||Theres a white 944 auto at the shop where my fiero is.  I think its a POS.  Nice to look at, horrible suspension, 2.4 Litre 4 cylender, not much performance wise.  EVen our stock Fieros would smoke them, Autocross or Drag.  Even the mechanics think so...  HE said "even you could afforc a porsche like this"  "no thanks, i dont want a porsche like that"<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan<BR>1986 GT Fastback, <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FIERO2.JPG">       <BR>Holley Ram-Air, Power Steering, K&N Filtercharger, Bosch Wires, Bosch Platinum Plugs, Conrad Distribuiter Cap/Rotor, New GT Seats, New Headliner, New Carpet, Ocelet Exhaust, Red Underbody Light Kit (coming soon)<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||08-28-2001||08:47 AM||||The latest issue of Grassroots Motorsports has an article titled "Affordable Porsches" and they discuss advantages/disadvantages of 928s.  Like the 944, they're a great car for the money IMHO.<P>I met a guy at Mid-Ohio a couple of years back with a nice 928 with 168K miles on it.  He said it had been very reliable except for the clutch, which he put in himself.  Said it wasn't too bad a job, just time-consuming...just took him all weekend.||||reg||14
Z||000006||firstfiero||08-28-2001||09:02 AM||||     I wouldn't say short lived....They started making the same year as the 924. I believe that was 1977 and were made to I believe 91 or 92. They died because the bug eyes and front engine never went over and they weren't as cheap as the 944. Believe me as a former 924 owner if your engine isn't in the rear the Porsche drivers don't respect you...Your not driving a REAL Porsche in their oppinion. The Boxster is the car that finnaly broke that barrier. <P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||spideyfoot||08-28-2001||09:56 AM||||got one love it. Just not many aftermarket parts. Well they have some but most of the books are leaned towards 944s and 911s. Lots of money to tune up. $120 plus for spark plug wires ... Check for bad water pump and see if you can see the timing belt make sure its good. They will smoke most Vetts and are a lot smaller in real life than they appear.||||reg||1
Z||000008||JSocha||08-28-2001||09:56 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.derporsche.net/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.derporsche.net/</A> <P>Go down to General Porceh Links and select MORE PORCHE SITES<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||12
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||08-28-2001||10:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Kasdan:<BR><B>Theres a white 944 auto at the shop where my fiero is.  I think its a POS.  Nice to look at, horrible suspension, 2.4 Litre 4 cylender, not much performance wise.  EVen our stock Fieros would smoke them, Autocross or Drag.  Even the mechanics think so...  HE said "even you could afforc a porsche like this"  "no thanks, i dont want a porsche like that"<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Haha That Horrbile suspension would drive circles around a 84 to 87 Fiero , as for an 88 it would come down to driver.<P>928's are nice , I suggest you check out excellence the Porsche magazine ,a few issues back they had a buyers guide to 928's. The newer the car you get the better off you are. They had problems with Iron waterpumps and aluminum blocks , this caused corrosion between the 2 and leakage.<P>The issue of excellence is the December 2000 one , it has the market updaye on the 928 , they reccomend the 87 and newer cars. I am seen a few come out to the track , there weight makes there brakes inadequate as the car is pushed harder and harder , but great power and balance.<P>Unfortunatley , most of them out there are automatics  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Hope this helps<P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000010||spideyfoot||08-28-2001||01:12 PM||||Mine is a 5 speed. Would be hard pressed to own an automatic. For 2 above its Porsche pronounced Por sha. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||ray b||08-28-2001||02:09 PM||||my rich buddy got a new 928 in 91 it was a POS alott of stuff broke on it and it was quickly traded in on a new 911 turbo. he has got 2 more turbos a 94 and a 95 twin turbo that he loves keap all 3 911's.<BR>parts are sky high priced for the 928 get a good runner not a project car or it will eat up cash like no other car.<BR>924-944 will handel better that most all other cars vetts and fiero's inc and sure will beat a fiero in a drag race 944t was 240 hp.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
