Z||000015||Slammed Fiero||08-28-2001||10:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rodmcneill:<BR><B>Thanks for the info guys. I learned a lot by checking out those sites. I didn't realise there were many model-engine size differences. The price of replacement parts is incredible. $120.00 for dist.cap-rotor, $120.00- $220.00 for ignition wires! The are really nice vehicles though.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I can only speak from having experienced a 911RS , $14 per plug , Dual fire motor , 12 plugs.<P>Scheduled maintenance can run 2 grand , but worth it, a porsche witha proper logged , stamped book is a valuable car.<P>You can call PNA (porsche North america) with a VIN # and they will tell you the details of the car , what it sold for, options ect. if you own the car , send them $30 and they will send you a build sheet and all sorts of info.<P>JM <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||dman||08-28-2001||11:58 PM||||Hello everyone. I usually don't post much but all this talk about Porsches has got me interested.<P>The Porsche 944 is truly one of the best handling cars built. Yeah, parts are a little expensive...but they're definitely cheaper than parts for a 928. The 944 can be had for a fair price but add-on performance comes at a dear price.<P>I have a 944 with a Chevy 4.3 liter v6. This way I can have awesome performance, incredible handling (the v6 isn't really that much heavier than the Porsche 4 cyl), without the expensive price.<P>I still need to wire up the ECM and harness onto the Porsche as well as a few other things so I can't give exact performance figures. If I had put a strong 350 in there instead than I've heard that "0-60 times should be around 4.5 to 5.0 seconds and 1/4 mile times should be approximately 12-13 seconds. These figures can be achieved without spinning your tires or abusing the clutch."<P>Putting a SBC V8 into a 928 is even easier and cheaper to do.<P>Check out Rengade Hybrids at <A HREF="http://www.renegadehybrids.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.renegadehybrids.com</A> <BR>for more info.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||AusFiero||08-29-2001||06:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rodmcneill:<BR><B>Thanks for the info guys. I learned a lot by checking out those sites. I didn't realise there were many model-engine size differences. The price of replacement parts is incredible. $120.00 for dist.cap-rotor, $120.00- $220.00 for ignition wires! The are really nice vehicles though.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Sound like the costs of Fiero parts in Australia  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
